Paul,
  After more wrangling than I'd hoped, I think I have managed to push a change to gbm forward, and enter it as a pull request.   This wouldn't have been possible without your fine help below; ssh keys and company servers created their own hurdles.

These changes have nothing to do with the variable names discussion.  They allow gbm to work directly with a data set such as my most recent fit, where y was missing on about 18/3000, multiple obs per subject, and a history number as the id, without having to create special intermediate datasets or variables.

   gbm( zscore ~ age + test + education, data=tmt, obs.id <http://obs.id>= clinic)

If I have succeeded (IF), there will be an update to R/gbm.r and a new file R/nagbm.r.  I'll send those directly as an attachment if you like, only to verify that I have correctly wrangled git and github.

Terry T.

On 11/22/2016 09:53 AM, Paul Metcalfe wrote:

> Ah, yes. git is (ahem) intuitive like that.
>
> The workflow is clone as one off
>
> Then branch, code, push to your repo, PR, back to master, fetch from gbm-developers/gbm, merge with gbm-developers/gbm/master, push to your master if you feel like it.
>
> In what-to-type detail, what you should first do (as a one-off, in your clone)
>
> git remote add upstream https://github.com/gbm-developers/gbm
>
> Then, for each new thing.
>
> git checkout -b patch/mynewstuff
>
> Code code code, committing as nesc
>
> git push -u origin/patch/mynewstuff
>
> Pull request (and wait!)
>
> git checkout master
>
> git fetch upstream
>
> git merge --ff-only upstream/master
>
> For more advanced use with temporary local files I'm told that git stash is the thing to do, but I just get confused when I try that...
>
> --
> pdm
>
